one would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (101). Topeka's all-time high is 114\u00b0F, on July 24, 1936.","t_chg":"Topeka's nights have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","p_chg":"Across 138 complete years: Topeka's average low shows no clear change: +0.03\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.04 to +0.11) that includes zero. Topeka's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.17\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.10 to +0.25), about +2.4\u00b0F since 1888. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.48\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.11 to +0.85) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.75\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.25 to +1.25). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 3 of 12 months. Highs rose in 4 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Topeka's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.26 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 36.5 inches a year. The wettest year was 1973 (60.89 in); the driest, 1963 (19.07 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 11 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}